  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x200B;&#x200B;Comprehensive Migration Data Initiative in the Western Hemisphere - The Lighthouse Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Migration in the Western Hemisphere is on the rise. In addition to historical migration patterns and as the result of many factors, including poverty, social exclusion, violence, political instability, the increasing impact of disasters exacerbated by the effects of climate change, food insecurity and the erroneous perception of open US borders, the region has experienced in recent years unprecedented movements, both in scale and complexity. 
 
In this context, tens of thousands of migrants find themselves in situations of great vulnerability due to dangerous conditions along the migratory route, gender-based violence, exploitation, abuse and/or violence at the hands of traffickers and smugglers, the lack of shelter, and limited access to health and mental health services, among others. Women, boys, and girls, especially if unaccompanied, people with disabilities and people of diverse sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, and sexual characteristics (SOGIESC) are the most exposed to these risks. 
 
Effective collective efforts to ensure safe orderly, and regular migration require robust data and evidence to support development of policies, projects, and programmes at local, national, and regional levels, as well as to deliver on commitments under global agreements. 
Unfortunately, to date the approach to migration data collection, management and analysis in the Americas has been fragmented, posing challenges to building a coherent and comprehensive evidence base and hence limiting the capacity to timely deliver numbers and characteristics as well as foresight for effective response and programming. 
 
The Lighthouse Programme aims to build coherent and comprehensive data systems at the continental level to generate timely and predictable data and analysis for action, insights, and foresight to inform decision-making on migration policy, programming, and initiatives in the Western Hemisphere. The Programme will bring together and work closely with countries and programmes with existing data collection and analysis exercise to build and systematize standards, coherence, and interoperability to enable continental level consolidation of datasets and analysis. It will also work with countries where new key data and information gaps are identified during the implementation. The first year of the Programme will produce consolidated continental datasets and foundational systems for enhancing a unified regional data framework and building a robust and sustainable data network throughout the Americas. The project will establish a continental platform, harmonize, and standardize data mechanisms and tools, and strengthening capacities on data collection, management, and analysis. 
 
The proposed workplan will enable the upscaling and consolidation of existing data sources by IOM, partners and governments that inform policymaking and operations, deepen insight, and inform anticipatory action by governments and other stakeholders. Data gaps will also be identified and addressed. Consistent and timely data, visualization, analysis, and information from this stronger evidence base will facilitate a more grounded, rights-based, and insightful approach to policy formulation and subsequent programming.</narrative>
  </description>
